    "body": "DATE OF SURGERY: [___]\n\nOPERATING SURGEON: [___]\nRESIDENT SURGEON(S) ASSISTING: [___]\nFIRST ASSISTANT: [___]\nLEVEL OF INVOLVEMENT OF ATTENDING SURGEON: Dr. [___] was present for the entire case and participated throughout.\n\nPREOPERATIVE DIAGNOSIS: [Chronic/recurrent tonsillitis / Tonsillar hypertrophy with OSA]\nPOSTOPERATIVE DIAGNOSIS: same\n\nOPERATION PERFORMED: Bilateral Tonsillectomy [+/- Adenoidectomy]\n\nINDICATION: [Age] [M/F] with a history of [___] who has failed medical management including [___] courses of antibiotics.\n\nFINDINGS: [Symmetric/asymmetric] tonsils, [size ___]\n\nOPERATIVE PROCEDURE: After written informed consent was obtained, the patient was brought to the operating room and placed supine. A time out was performed with [____]. After induction of general endotracheal anesthesia, the patient was prepped and draped in sterile fashion. The bed was turned 90 degrees, a shoulder roll was placed, and the neck extended. A Crowe-Davis mouth gag was inserted and the tonsils exposed bilaterally. Bovie cautery was set to [___]. The right tonsil was grasped with a curved Allis clamp and retracted medially; a subcapsular dissection was performed with Bovie cautery from superior to inferior until the tonsil was fully removed, transected at the junction of the lingual and palatine tonsils, with the anterior and posterior pillars left intact. Hemostasis was achieved with suction cautery. The left tonsil was removed in a similar fashion. The oropharynx was irrigated copiously with normal saline and both tonsillar beds inspected for hemostasis; no active bleeding was noted. Care was returned to anesthesia and the patient awoke without apparent complication, transferred to recovery in stable condition.\n\nANESTHESIA: General endotracheal anesthesia\nBLOOD LOSS: [___] cc\nDRAINS AND PACKS: None\nWOUND CLASSIFICATION: [Clean / Clean-contaminated / Contaminated / Dirty]\nCOMPLICATIONS: None\nPROGNOSIS: Good\nDISPOSITION: To recovery room"
